        "problem": "Solve the inequality $4-{e}^{x+1}>6.$",
        "worked_solution": "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-{e}^{x+1} & >6 \\\\ -{e}^{x+1} & >2 \\\\ {e}^{x+1} & <-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{e}^{x+1}>0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality ${e}^{x+1}<-2$ has no solutions.",
